<h2>Is Betano Legitimate in Peru?</h2>
<p><strong>Strong industry corroboration, no direct regulator-registry confirmation:</strong> Betano's MINCETUR licensing (Directorial Resolutions No. 1540-2024 and No. 1541-2024-MINCETUR/VMT/DGJCMT, reportedly granted 27 March 2024 to Kaizen Gaming Perú S.A.C.) is widely and consistently reported across Peru-specific industry and press sources, with matching resolution numbers and dates, and its status as the first operator ever licensed in Peru is a specific, checkable claim rather than a vague one. CasinoRadar could not query MINCETUR's own registry (apuestasdeportivas.mincetur.gob.pe, directly reachable but without a queryable structured operator list during this research) to independently confirm the specific line item, and betano.pe itself returned HTTP 403 on every direct-fetch attempt — so this review could not upgrade licensing to a direct regulator-page "Verified" status.</p>

<h2>Licensing &amp; Regulation</h2>
<p>Peru's Ley 31557 (published 13 August 2022), as amended by Ley 31806, created a dedicated legal framework for distance gaming and distance sports betting, regulated by MINCETUR's Dirección General de Juegos de Casino y Máquinas Tragamonedas (DGJCMT) — the same framework already established via CasinoRadar's Inkabet review. Unlike Mexico, where online gambling operations must run as an extension of an existing land-based casino permit, Peru's framework allows standalone remote-gaming licenses, which Betano is reported to have been the first operator to obtain.</p>

<h2>Responsible Gambling</h2>
<p>Reported: Betano offers temporary account closures (24 hours, 48 hours, 7 days, or 30 days) and longer-term self-exclusion (6, 12, 24 months, or indefinitely), managed through the player's account settings. This sits alongside Peru's Ley 31557/31806 baseline requirements, which mandate deposit limits and self-exclusion tools and bar participation by minors and by individuals excluded by judicial order. Peru has no centralized, state-run self-exclusion registry of the kind confirmed elsewhere in this project for US states and European markets — self-exclusion is offered per-operator rather than through a national system. This reporting rests on indexed Betano policy content and independent legal-industry sourcing rather than a live fetch of betano.pe, which returned HTTP 403 during this research.</p>

<h2>Bonuses</h2>
<p>Reported, with a caveat: a 100% deposit match up to 1,000 PEN for the casino product and a 50% match up to 500 PEN for the sportsbook are the most consistently-cited figures across independent Peru-specific sources. Other sources describe a distinct 80-free-spins casino offer or a "S/2,000 + S/60" figure — these read as separate, concurrent promo-code-activated offers rather than a hard conflict with the standard deposit-match figures, but were not independently confirmed for this review. <strong>Always verify the current bonus terms directly on betano.pe.</strong></p>

<h2>Games</h2>
<p>Reported: approximately 2,200 slot titles and a broader catalog cited at around 3,000 games in total, across providers including Pragmatic Play, Evolution, Novomatic, iSoftBet, Play'n GO, NetEnt, Playson, Red Tiger, and Playtech. Live-dealer tables from Evolution and Pragmatic Play are also reported. This is a more consistent and specific sourcing picture than CasinoRadar's earlier Inkabet review achieved for its game catalog, corroborated across multiple independent Peru-specific comparison sites rather than just one or two.</p>

<h2>Payment Methods</h2>
<p>Reported: Yape, Plin, PagoEfectivo, and direct bank transfers (BBVA and other major Peruvian banks) for deposits, with a minimum deposit of S/20. Yape and Plin deposits are reported as near-instant, reflecting in the account balance within about a minute. Yape withdrawals are reported with a minimum of S/50 and a maximum of S/3,500 per day. None of this was confirmed against betano.pe directly, which returned HTTP 403 during this research.</p>

